  ceiling gain of the oracle over 90               : -11

  The probe's premise, checked against the archive:
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (a) 'the 19-dim row is blind at tick 1' -- CONFIRMED but for a different reason than
      assumed: 16 of 17 dimensions have exactly one value (only #13 moves), #17 is
      constant at 12.0, and #11 saturates at +1.0 because every tick-1 deadline is
      longer than the 50-step urgency scale.
  (b) 'the tick-1 cohort is roughly 50/50' -- REFUTED. It is 69 on-time out of 71.
      The AUC there is unmeasurable for lack of negatives, not uninformative at 0.5.
  (c) 'new physics features would separate tick-1 targets' -- NOT TESTABLE, because
      (b) leaves nothing to separate. On the whole 192 the best is A at 0.908, but A
      correlates 0.75 with detection time, which alone reaches 0.019.
  (d) 'a Top-K selector could beat 90/192' -- NOT answerable from this archive, and the
      naive oracle would mislead. Ranking by the observed label at K=12 keeps 80, and
      the best possible over all 24 keeps 80 -- but those labels were realized under
      the learned arm's own sends, so an oracle over them can never exceed what that
      arm achieved. normal_immediate's 90/192 comes from a different world entirely,
      which is proof that a better allocation exists but not proof of its ceiling.
      What the sweep does show is the shape: K=11 -> 79, K=12 -> 80, K=13 -> 80,
      K=16 -> 80. The curve is still climbing at the affordable K, so the binding
      constraint is the send count, not the ranking.

  The one comparison this archive does license, arm to arm on the same 192:
    normal_immediate 90 sends -> 90 on-time; learned 91 sends -> 80 on-time.
    Immediate is ahead by 10 tasks on nearly identical spend, so the learned policy's
    problem is not the budget alone -- it is which targets it buys with it.

  DECISION against the two criteria stated before the probe ran:
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Criterion A ('AUC >= 0.65 at tick 1 AND a Top-K ceiling well above 90/192' ->
  environment is already separable, just swap the dims and retrain):
    * tick-1 AUC >= 0.65: reached only by a feature with 2 negatives out of 71, and
      the best first-order value (A, 0.73) collapses once detection time is held fixed.
      At ticks 2-10, where both classes exist, the best is 0.680.
    * Top-K ceiling well above 90/192: NOT reachable at this budget. The label oracle
      at K=12 keeps 80, and the answer to 'could a better ranking have done more' is
      'no better than what the arm already did, in the worlds the arm created'.
    => Criterion A is NOT met. Swapping in these four features would not have moved
       the P4 score, because the loss is spent budget, not missed ranking.

  Criterion B ('even with real distance and unclipped slack the AUC stays ~0.50,
  so all targets are physically alike and the outcome is downstream lottery'):
    * Physically the targets are NOT alike: feasibility is 192/192, so no target is
      ruled out by geometry, and the spread in work and slack is real.
    * But the outcomes are also NOT a lottery among equals: on_time equals the number
      of DISTINCT targets billed, exactly, in 16 of 16 worlds. The deciding variable
      is not a target property at all -- it is whether that target got a first send.
    => Criterion B is also NOT met as stated, but its conclusion holds for a different
       reason than either branch anticipated.

  What the evidence actually supports, stated at its own strength:
    The binding constraint in these 8 scenes is that 58,600 B at N=12 bills 11-12 of
    24 targets, and every distinct target billed finishes on time. A policy's score is
    therefore determined almost entirely by (a) not wasting sends on duplicates and
    (b) covering as many distinct targets as the budget allows. The learned arm wasted
    11 sends on duplicates and covered 80 distinct targets; immediate wasted 0 and
    covered 90. The 10-task gap is that difference, and no feature from a richer
    state can close it. Only a change to what an emission IS (one send per distinct
    target, i.e. a sender-side dedupe), or to the budget/target ratio, moves it.

  Recommendation, and this is a change of plan: do NOT open full_features on the
  strength of this probe. The probe cannot distinguish 'the policy never learned to
  dedupe' from 'the policy was never given the chance', but it shows the reward is
  dominated by a dedupe/coverage term that is already representable in the 19 dims
  (#2 sent_count, #12 urgency). The informative next experiment is a dedupe control:
  forbid re-sending a target that is already on the air, and see whether the learned
  arm then matches immediate. If it does, the 10-task gap was pure duplicate waste and
  the separability question is moot at this operating point. If it does not, the
  ranking question comes back and full_features earns a real test.
  Either way this does not substitute for the deadline-separable world if the goal is
  to study target SELECTION: at 11 sends against 24 always-feasible targets, selection
  has almost no room to express itself.

  What this licenses: the environment as generated is NOT separable in the way the
  full_features plan assumed, but the reason is the budget-to-target ratio plus a
  detection schedule that front-loads the easy tasks -- not per-target physics.
  What it does not license: any claim that full_features would or would not help, or
  that a deadline-separable world would move the ceiling. Both need new worlds.
  See the DECISION block above for the recommended next step.
